# The dynamic recompiler (offsets 10000-182FB).

@0EA2C dr_translate_stub
    Fixed-position branch into the recompiler's code block.
@0EA40 dr_translate_stub_2

@10000 dr_translate
    ===========================================================================
    Dynamic recompiler: translator
    ===========================================================================
    Translate the block of 68K code starting at pc - 2 into PowerPC code in
    the translation cache.  Entered from dr_translate_miss with cr_dr_busy
    set; leaves through dr_translate_done.

    The translator borrows three emulator registers for the duration:
        blk_ofs (r23)   byte offset of the current 68K instruction in the block
        rec_ptr (r29)   current instruction record
        out_ptr (r30)   last word written to the translation cache
    base_disp addresses the translator's tables.  The emulator's own values
    of disp_table and code_ptr are reloaded from the state block on the way
    out.

    Translation is three passes over an array of 12-byte records, one per
    68K instruction:

        +0  flags (which condition codes the instruction reads and writes,
            branch-target and end-of-block markers)
        +2  offset of the instruction from the start of the block
        +4  emitter: offset of the code generator for this instruction

    Pass 1 (dr_scan_next) decodes forward, one record per instruction, until
    an instruction that ends the block or 120 bytes of 68K code.
    Pass 2 (dr_liveness_loop) walks the records backwards working out which
    instructions compute condition codes that nothing uses, so that cheaper
    code can be emitted for them.
    Pass 3 (dr_emit_next) calls each record's emitter, which appends PowerPC
    instructions to the cache.

    Every loop polls cr_special_event_pending so that a long translation
    cannot hold off an interrupt; an interrupted translation is abandoned.
10000 pc = address of the first instruction of the block
10004 rec_ptr = start of the record array
10020 base of the pass-1 decoders and their tables

@10034 dr_scan_advance
    Pass 1: the instruction just decoded is rtn_addr bytes longer than its
    opcode word.
@10038 dr_scan_store
    Pass 1: finish the record for the instruction just decoded
    (out_ptr = emitter offset, immed_data = flags) and move to the next.
10048 a block is at most 120 bytes of 68K code
@1005C dr_scan_next
    Pass 1: start a record and call the decoder for this opcode's line.
1005C prefetch_data = opcode of the instruction at blk_ofs
10064 (opcode >> 12) * 4
10068 table of sixteen decoders in the state block
10070 cr4..cr7 = opcode[15:0], for the decoder to test
@10080 dr_scan_block_full
    The block reached its size limit: end it with code that re-enters the
    recompiler at the following instruction.
@100C8 dr_scan_end_block
    Pass 1: the instruction just decoded ends the block.

@100FC dr_liveness
    Pass 2: backwards over the records, clearing the "must compute" flag
    bits of instructions whose condition codes are overwritten before
    anything reads them.
@10150 dr_liveness_loop

@1018C dr_emit_next
    Pass 3: call the emitter for the next record.  A non-negative emitter
    value is an offset from dr_emitters in this block; a negative one
    selects a routine relative to a table in RAM.
101A8 record +4 now holds the instruction's offset in the translated block
101B8 prefetch_data = the opcode again

@101DC dr_fix_branches
    After emission: resolve branches between instructions of the block.

@10200 dr_emit_finish
    Flush the new code out of the data cache and into the instruction
    cache, then hand the block to the run-time.
@10234 dr_translate_abort
    A special event became pending during translation.  Give up, restore
    the borrowed registers and let the interpreter handle the event.

@10280 dr_line_0
    Pass-1 decoders, one per value of opcode bits 15:12, each 64 bytes long
    so that the table of sixteen addresses in the state block can be built
    with a loop (see dr_setup).  A decoder works out the length of the
    instruction from the addressing mode tables that follow the decoders,
    chooses an emitter and the condition code flags, and returns to
    dr_scan_store or dr_scan_end_block.  Opcodes the recompiler does not
    translate end the block with a return to the interpreter.

@11038 dr_ea_length_tables
    Byte tables indexed by a 6-bit effective address field (and by the
    MOVE destination field) giving the number of extension bytes; the low
    bit flags modes that need the extension word itself to be examined.

@11338 dr_emitters
    Pass-3 code generators.  Emitter values stored in the records are
    offsets from this label.

@1767C dr_templates
    Instruction templates.  These words are never executed here: emitters
    load them as data, fill in a displacement or immediate, and store them
    in the translation cache.
1767C advance pc by a 16-bit amount
17680 advance pc by the high half of a 32-bit amount
17684 call into the run-time
17688 conditional exit when a special event is pending
17690 unconditional branch
17694 unconditional branch (used when chaining blocks)

@1782C dr_exit_to_interpreter
    Leave translated code (or abandon a lookup) and continue in the
    interpreter with the instruction at pc - 2.
1782C prefetch_data = that instruction's opcode
17838 restore dr_entry
1783C prefetch the word after it

@178A0 dr_chain_block
    The target of a block-ending branch has been found in the lookup table.
    Overwrite the call that brought control here with a direct branch to
    the target block, so that next time the two blocks run back to back
    without entering the run-time at all.
178B0 template: unconditional branch
178C0 patch the call site
178C8 make the patched word visible to instruction fetch

@178E0 dr_indirect_exit
    Entered from translated code at a branch whose target is only known at
    run time (RTS, JMP (An), ...).  The two words following the call are a
    one-entry cache: the 68K target seen last time and its translation.
178FC cached 68K address
17904 cached translation
1791C same target as last time: go straight there

@17940 dr_promote_hit
    Lookup hit in a slot other than the first: swap the entry to the front
    of its set before jumping to it.

@17964 dr_dispatch
    ===========================================================================
    Dynamic recompiler: run-time dispatch
    ===========================================================================
    The interpreter comes here (through dr_entry, when cr_dr_active is set)
    after every instruction that changes the flow of control, with LR
    holding the dispatch table entry it would otherwise have used.

    If a translation of the code at pc - 2 exists, run it; otherwise
    translate it first.  Returning through LR instead simply interprets the
    instruction.

    The lookup table maps 68K addresses to translated code.  It is indexed
    by the low eleven bits of pc; each index selects sixteen bytes (two
    {68K address, code address} pairs) and a search continues into the
    following sixteen, so an address can be found in any of four slots.
17964 opcode of the instruction about to run
17970 0xAAFE: never translated
17974 0xFE02: never translated
17984 a whole-cache flush is in progress
17988 address ranges are waiting to be invalidated
@17994 dr_lookup
17994 lookup table base (64 KB aligned)
17998 insert (pc & 0x7FF) << 4
179A8 odd address: let the interpreter raise the error
179B8 hit in the first slot
179E0 miss: remember where the search ended

@179EC dr_flush_step
    Discard every translation.  The lookup table is cleared 2 KB at a time
    and the page bitmap 2 KB at a time, checking for special events between
    chunks; progress is kept in the state block so the work resumes on the
    next dispatch.
@17A84 dr_flush_done

@17AC0 dr_invalidate_step
    Service one pending range-invalidation request: scan part of the lookup
    table and drop every translation whose 68K address lies in the range.
    Requests are flagged in a bit mask that is updated with lwarx/stwcx.
    because it is also written from outside the emulator's normal flow.
@17BE8 dr_drop_entry
    Remove one lookup table entry and overwrite the first instruction of
    its translation with a branch to dr_stale_block, in case another block
    is chained directly to it.

@17C8C dr_stale_block
    Entered from a translation that has been invalidated: look the address
    up afresh (which will retranslate it).

@17CD8 dr_translate_miss
    No translation exists.  Choose a slot in the set (an empty one if there
    is one, otherwise evict the last), then translate.
17D54 the translator now owns blk_ofs, rec_ptr and out_ptr
@17D5C dr_translate_done
    The translator has produced a block.  Enter it in the lookup table,
    note in the page bitmap that this piece of 68K memory has been
    translated, and run it.  If the cache has filled up, start a flush and
    interpret instead.
17D70 slot: code address
17D74 slot: 68K address
17D88 past the end of the cache?
17DAC word index: one word per 16 KB of 68K memory
17DB4 bit number: one bit per 512 bytes

@17E00 dr_invalidate_range
    Request invalidation of the translations for a range of 68K memory
    (addr = start, data = length).  Called for the 68K cache-flush
    operations, i.e. whenever 68K software announces that it has written
    code.

@17FA0 dr_request_flush
    Ask for the whole translation cache to be discarded.
@17FBC dr_reset_requests

@17FFC dr_fault_fixup
    Called while an exception frame is being built.  If the faulting
    PowerPC instruction is inside the translation cache, work out the 68K
    address it corresponds to so that the frame holds a 68K pc.

@181AC dr_setup
    Carve up the memory given to the recompiler (base and size are in the
    state block): lookup table (64 KB), a RAM table, the invalidation
    request table, the translation cache, the page bitmap at the top, and
    2 KB for instruction records.  Then install the recompiler's entry
    points in the state block in place of the stubs.
181E8 address of the kernel's information record
18250 dr_dispatch
18258 dr_request_flush
18260 dr_invalidate_range
18268 dr_fault_fixup
18280 dr_line_0: the sixteen pass-1 decoders are 64 bytes apart

@182A8 dr_credits

@180C4 dr_fault_redirect
    Called while a bus error frame is being built for a fault inside
    translated code or inside the translator.
@180F8 dr_resume_in_translator
@180FC dr_resume_after_fault
@181A8 dr_null_hook
